.TH SENSOR-DHT11 1 "September 2026" "sensor-dht11 2.4.2" "WildlifeSystems"
.SH NAME
sensor-dht11 \- read DHT11 temperature and humidity sensors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sensor-dht11
[\fB\-\-version\fR|\fB\-v\fR|\fBversion\fR|\fBidentify\fR|\fBlist\fR|\fBsetup\fR|\fBenable\fR|\fBmock\fR|\fBtemperature\fR|\fBhumidity\fR|\fBinternal\fR|\fBexternal\fR|\fBall\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sensor-dht11
reads DHT11 temperature and humidity sensors connected to the GPIO pins of a
Raspberry Pi, through the Linux GPIO character device, and outputs the readings
as a JSON array in the WildlifeSystems format, one reading per measurement. It
is normally run by
.BR sr (1).
.SH COMMANDS
Not specifying a command is equivalent to \fBall\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-version, \-v, version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B identify
Exit with code 60, identifying the program as a WildlifeSystems sensor driver.
.TP
.B list
List the measurements provided, one per line: temperature and humidity. Each
is also accepted as a command.
.TP
.B temperature, humidity
Output only that measurement from every sensor.
.TP
.B setup, enable
Report that nothing is required. The sensor is read by bit-banging a GPIO pin,
so no device-tree overlay has to be enabled. The commands are provided so that
every driver answers the same commands.
.TP
.B mock
Output fixed readings in the real output format, for testing without hardware:
22.0\[de]C and 55% humidity, at location \fI{{node}}\fR.
.TP
.B internal
Output only readings from sensors configured as internal.
.TP
.B external
Output only readings from sensors configured as external.
.TP
.B all
Output every measurement from every sensor.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file
.I /etc/ws/sensors/dht11.json
is a JSON array with one object per sensor. The package installs it with one
sensor on GPIO 4, inside the enclosure. Without the file, a single sensor on
GPIO 4 is read, and is treated as outside the enclosure. Each object may
contain the following fields.
.TP
.B pin
The GPIO pin number, 2 to 27. The default is 4. An invalid pin number is
replaced by the default.
.TP
.B internal
Whether the sensor is inside the enclosure. The default is false.
.TP
.B sensor_id
A custom sensor identifier. If omitted, the identifier is
\fI<serial>_dht11\fR, where \fI<serial>\fR is the Raspberry Pi serial number.
Where more than one entry omits it, each is instead
\fI<serial>_dht11_pin<N>\fR, so that two sensors do not share an identifier.
Each measurement appends its own name, giving for example
\fI<serial>_dht11_temperature\fR.
.TP
.B sensor_name
A human-readable name, reported in the \fBsensor_name\fR field.
.TP
.B location
Where the sensor is: the string \fI{{node}}\fR for the position of the node,
\fI{{none}}\fR for a sensor that has no position, or an object with
\fBlatitude\fR and \fBlongitude\fR in decimal degrees and, optionally,
\fBaltitude\fR and \fBaccuracy\fR in metres. If omitted, the \fBlocation\fR
field of the reading is null.
.PP
An example configuration:
.PP
.nf
[
  {
    "pin": 4,
    "internal": true,
    "sensor_name": "Enclosure DHT11",
    "location": "{{node}}"
  }
]
.fi
.SH OUTPUT
A JSON array with one reading per measurement of each sensor. Each reading has
the fields of the
.BR sc-prototype (1)
template. The fields filled in by this driver are as follows.
.TP
.B sensor
"dht11_temperature" or "dht11_humidity".
.TP
.B device
"dht11".
.TP
.B measures
"temperature" or "humidity".
.TP
.B unit
"Celsius" or "percentage".
.TP
.B value
The reading, to one decimal place, or null if the reading failed.
.TP
.B error
The reason the reading failed, or null. A reading never has both a value and
an error.
.TP
.B sensor_id, sensor_name, internal, location
Taken from the configuration, as described above.
.TP
.B timestamp
The Unix time at which the sensor was read.
.PP
The \fBnode_id\fR and \fBdeployment_id\fR fields are left null and are filled
in by
.BR sr (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ws/sensors/dht11.json
The configuration file.
.TP
.I /dev/gpiochip0
The GPIO character device through which the sensor is read. Version 2 of its
interface is used, which requires Linux 5.10 or later.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 20
An invalid argument, or the readings could not be produced at all, for example
because \fBsc-prototype\fR was unavailable. Nothing is printed in that case.
.TP
.B 60
The \fBidentify\fR command.
.SH NOTES
A failed read is retried with increasing delays, within a budget of 8 seconds
per invocation shared between the configured sensors. The budget keeps the
program inside the 10-second limit that
.BR sr (1)
imposes, so a sensor that cannot be read is still reported, with the reason in
its \fBerror\fR field, rather than the program being killed before it can say
so.
.PP
A watchdog timer (30 seconds) is a last resort for GPIO operations that hang
outright; the retry budget expires long before it.
.PP
The binary uses SCHED_FIFO real-time scheduling during GPIO reads to minimise
preemption-related timing failures. The
.B cap_sys_nice
capability is set on the binary at install time.
.PP
GPIO pins are validated to be in the range 2-27 (valid Raspberry Pi GPIO pins).
Invalid pins in the configuration file will be replaced with the default pin (4).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sr (1),
.BR sc-prototype (1),
.BR sensor-bme680 (1),
.BR sensor-w1therm (1)
